## 9. Candidate airport cases and quantitative exhibits

### 9.1 Airport cases the article can build on

- **San Francisco (SFO), 2018–2020 — the mechanism case.** SFO physically disabled airline-facing terminal-wide paging capability and centralized non-routine announcements. Individual paging fell from 492 to 261 daily; cumulative announcement duration from 145 to 58 minutes (E-0032, E-0054). Cite the counts (attributed to SFO reporting via Airport Technology feature), not the 40% figure. Mechanism records: E-0033 (ops-analyst), E-0120 (airline-strategist), E-0174 (COO).
- **London City Airport, 2008–present — the policy case.** Silent-terminal policy documented on LCY's own operations pages; PA reserved for bad weather delays or genuine emergencies (E-0058 technology-scout, E-0036 ops-analyst, E-0245 virtual-pat). Behavioral premise (frequent flyers wear headphones on entry) is documented in Smithsonian coverage cited in the contrarian brief.
- **Amsterdam Schiphol — the boundary case.** Silent-airport claim; 48-hour pre-notification for deaf and blind passengers (E-0098); illustrates what quiet-airport policy looks like in a jurisdiction with a different accessibility regime, and why direct transfer to the US fails.
- **Helsinki Vantaa (Finavia) — the operator-negotiation case.** Silent since 2015 (E-0037 ops-analyst, E-0016 infrastructure-economist); Finavia-referenced 2016 study found passengers judged the policy "more positive than neutral" (E-0038). Suppressed routine flight announcements from a 2019 rollout per subsequent reporting.
- **Dubai International — the volume-cap case.** Announcements from more than 2,000 daily to fewer than 700; two-per-flight cap; ten-month negotiation with customer airlines (E-0030, E-0031, E-0057). Airport operator claim, not independent measurement.
- **Denver International — the carrier-veto case.** Southwest reportedly blocked a proposed quiet-zone initiative (E-0121). The single strongest carrier-response data point in the ledger for MWAA's context.
- **Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood (FLL), January 6, 2017 — the stampede/communications-integrity case.** Five killed initially; ~90 minutes later, false-gunfire rumor triggered self-evacuation across four terminals with 50+ additional hospitalizations (E-0192, E-0201). PERF/National Policing Institute AAR is the primary reference.
- **Los Angeles (LAX), November 1, 2013 — the speed-of-response case.** TSO Gerardo Hernandez killed at checkpoint; TSA mandated annual active-shooter training in response (E-0193). LAX Airport Police Chief Gannon testimony: response speed is the operative variable (E-0194).
- **Atlanta (ATL), December 17, 2017 — the backup-power case.** 11-hour power outage; ~1,500 flights grounded; tower kept power, terminal did not (E-0204, E-0205). Argues for ECS head-end UPS runtime, not just terminal generator runtime.
- **MSP International — the accessibility precedent.** Hearing loops through Terminal 1 departures, arrivals, baggage claim, international arrivals, common-use gates E1–E4, Concourse G; T2 gates H11–H14 complete and H1–H10 in construction (E-0069 technology-scout). Real-time captioning of PA to gate-area screens (E-0247 virtual-pat).
- **Port Authority of NY & NJ — the mandate precedent.** Port Authority moved from encouragement to mandate for hearing loops at gates and information counters in new or significantly improved terminals (E-0070). The nearest US precedent for the kind of Design Manual amendment MWAA is being asked to write.
- **Pittsburgh International (PIT) — the sensory-space precedent.** 1,500 sq ft sensory space with decommissioned American Airlines cabin, mock jetway (E-0067).

### 9.2 Quantitative exhibits the article should carry forward from the quant brief

The Strategist should treat these as chart-ready:

- **Exhibit E1 — Design-time vs. retrofit cost delta:** basic acoustic ceiling $5–$15/sf new construction vs. $18–$45/sf retrofit; applied to Concourse E's 435,000 sf envelope, avoided rework range $1.3M–$17.4M (midpoint ~$6.8M). *Directly refutes the "affordability" objection.*
- **Exhibit E3 — Intelligibility criteria:** STI ≥ 0.50 average per ADS, ≥ 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations, SNR ≥ 10 dB(A), commissioned at opening and re-verified every 5 years or after material acoustic change. *One-paragraph adoptable design-manual language.*
- **Exhibit E4 — Announcement-density arithmetic:** typical US-hub boarding call sequence 6 announcements/gate/hour × 20 sec = 3.3% duty cycle; Helsinki/LCY-style final-call + all-zones-open ≈ 1.1% duty cycle; ~67% PA-time reduction. *Marked ASSUMPTION-driven pending MWAA telemetry.*
- **Exhibit E5 — Cost of redundant channels:** MWAA-wide gate-area FIDS redundancy ~198 gates at $1.6M–$3.6M capex; mobile notification platform ~$500K/year opex ($2.5M five-year TCO); combined five-year range $4.1M–$6.1M. *0.02%–0.03% of the announced IAD program.*
