## 10. Curator's summary

The swarm produced a factually strong, thematically consistent body of evidence with two structural weaknesses. First, half the "silent airport" precedent stack cannot support the weight the article's title implies — SFO's numbers are self-reported, the European peers do not publish measurements, and Denver's veto is a warning MWAA cannot dismiss. Second, the ledger contains zero explicit contradictions before this pass, which understates the real disagreements between the operator-executive chairs (CEO, COO, Public Safety, EM Director) and the passenger-experience advocates. This map surfaces both.

The article that survives is one that (a) reframes the standard as intelligibility-by-design anchored to ACRP 175, NFPA 72, and IEC 60268-16; (b) treats the Design Manual as the correct vehicle; (c) writes the recommendation in performance-spec terms that airlines can neither argue about nor circumvent; (d) sequences operational-governance moves against tenant audio explicitly, rather than promising them as byproducts; and (e) presents the SFO precedent as a mechanism, not a number.

The article that will not survive fact-check is one that leans on the 40% figure, treats "silent airport" as a legal category, promises safety improvements through fewer announcements, or implies the NTSB report addressed MWAA terminal-side communications.

The window to act runs until Concourse C/D scoping hardens. That is inside the current Board officer term. The Strategist should treat that window as the real deadline, not the article's own publication date.
